<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!--
      The theme the Activity runs under once the app is up.

      DayNight rather than Light: the app follows the system, and a hard-coded
      Light parent makes the window flash white behind the WebView on a phone in
      dark mode during a rotation or a configuration change.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
    </style>

    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="AppTheme">
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The launch theme.

      Android 12 and newer always draw their own splash screen; an app cannot
      opt out, and drawing a second one on top is the flicker every "why does my
      app blink" bug report is about. So this hands the system what it needs and
      nothing more:

      * `windowSplashScreenBackground` — the app's warm ground, matching the
        launcher tile the icon just animated out of.
      * `windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` — the splash artwork's own centre.

        This has been three things. Unset, the system used the *launcher* icon,
        so a cold start was the mascot tile, then the splash, then the app.
        Then it was `logo-symbol.png` — the flat orange with ㄱㄴㄷ — on the
        argument that it was "the splash's own mark". It was not: the approved
        splash artwork is a wordmark on a soft peach wash and carries no orange
        anywhere, so the cold start was an orange nobody else in the app draws,
        then the artwork, then the app. A customer photographed it and called
        the orange the old logo.

        `mipmap/splash_icon` is now a feathered cut of the approved artwork's
        own centre circle, generated by `scripts/content/build_app_icons.py`
        from the same file the splash bitmaps come from. The system draws that
        circle on the artwork's ground; the activity's first frame draws the
        whole artwork with the same circle in the same place; the wordmark and
        jamo appear on a frame the learner is already looking at. One splash.
        `scripts/check-native-splash.mjs` proves no other mark is referenced
        or packaged.
      * `postSplashScreenTheme` — what the Activity becomes once the splash is
        dismissed.

      `android:windowBackground` is the pre-Android-12 path, where there is no
      system splash and the window background *is* the splash.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splashBackground</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@mipmap/splash_icon</item>
        <!-- Still, not animated: there is nothing to animate and a duration
             here only delays the app behind a mark that is not moving. -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration">0</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/splash</item>
    </style>
</resources>
